  You are an expert in TypeScript, Node.js, Next.js App Router, React, tRPC, Shadcn UI, Radix UI, Tailwind, Better-auth and Drizzle.
  
  Code Style and Structure
  - Write concise, technical TypeScript code with accurate examples.
  - Use functional and declarative programming patterns; avoid classes.
  - Prefer iteration and modularization over code duplication.
  - Use descriptive variable names with auxiliary verbs (e.g., isLoading, hasError).
  - Structure files: exported component, subcomponents, helpers, static content, types.
  
  Naming Conventions
  - Use lowercase with dashes for directories (e.g., components/auth-wizard).
  - Also use kebab-case naming convention for component file names e.g. components/ai-workflow-dialog.tsx
  - Favor named exports for components.
  
  TypeScript Usage
  - Use TypeScript for all code; prefer interfaces over types.
  - Avoid enums; use maps instead.
  - Use functional components with TypeScript interfaces.
  - Avoid the use of 'any' type
  
  Syntax and Formatting
  - Use the "function" keyword for pure functions.
  - Avoid unnecessary curly braces in conditionals; use concise syntax for simple statements.
  - Use declarative JSX.
  
  UI and Styling
  - Use Shadcn UI, Radix, and Tailwind for components and styling.
  - Use Shadcn UI components wherever possible and store them under `src/components/ui`
  - Implement responsive design with Tailwind CSS; use a mobile-first approach.
  
  Performance Optimization
  - Minimize 'use client', 'useEffect', and 'setState'; favor React Server Components (RSC).
  - Wrap client components in Suspense with fallback.
  - Use dynamic loading for non-critical components.
  - Optimize images: use WebP format, include size data, implement lazy loading.
  
  Key Conventions
  - Optimize Web Vitals (LCP, CLS, FID).
  - Limit 'use client':
    - Favor server components and Next.js SSR.
    - Use only for Web API access in small components.
    - Avoid for data fetching or state management.

  tRPC Usage
  - Use tRPC for end-to-end typesafe APIs without code generation or runtime bloat.
  - Define procedures in routers that represent collections of related procedures with shared namespaces.
  - Structure routers by domain (e.g., users, posts, messages) and merge them into a centralized appRouter.
  - Use Zod for input validation in all procedures.
  - Leverage tRPC's wrapper for @tanstack/react-query to utilize typed hooks with full inference.
  - Export only router type definitions, never import server code on the client.
  - Use publicProcedure for public endpoints and create custom procedures with middleware for protected routes.
  - Implement proper error handling using TRPCError and the built-in error formatter for Zod validation errors.
  - Use superjson as the data transformer to preserve data types (Date, Map, Set) across client-server boundaries.
  
  tRPC Best Practices
  - Use meaningful procedure names that clearly indicate their purpose (e.g., getById, createPost, updateUser)
  - Chain .query() for data fetching, .mutation() for data modification, and .subscription() for real-time updates
  - Implement middleware for cross-cutting concerns (auth, logging, rate limiting)
  - Use context for shared dependencies (database connections, session data, services)
  - Prefer batched requests using httpBatchLink for better performance
  - Implement optimistic updates for better UX where data correctness allows
  - Use inferProcedureInput and inferProcedureOutput for type inference in tests
  - Keep procedures focused and single-purpose; compose complex operations from multiple procedures
  - Use .invalidate() to refresh queries after mutations
  - Leverage .setData() for optimistic updates with proper rollback in onError

  Drizzle ORM Best Practices
  - Use type-safe queries with Drizzle's query builder API.
  - Leverage Drizzle's relational queries for efficient data fetching with proper joins.
  - Define schemas using pgTable, pgEnum, and appropriate column types from drizzle-orm/pg-core.
  - Use generated columns (generatedAlwaysAsIdentity) for auto-incrementing primary keys.
  - Implement proper relations using the relations API for type-safe joins.
  - Use prepared statements for frequently executed queries to improve performance.
  - Leverage Drizzle's migration system for schema changes; always generate migrations before applying.
  - Use pnpm db:push for local db changes; use pnpm db:migrate:prod for pushing migrations to prod 
  - Use transactions for operations that require atomicity.
  - Prefer select() with explicit column selection over select() for better type inference.
  - Use SQL helpers (sql template tag) sparingly and only when necessary for complex queries.
  - Keep schema definitions in a centralized schema file with clear organization.
  - Use timestamps helper functions for created_at and updated_at columns when applicable.
  - For schema changes never manually create/update the sql migration files, rather update the schema.ts and run db:generate script 

  Ensure that pnpm is the default package manager for install dependencies
  
  Follow Next.js docs for Data Fetching, Rendering, and Routing.

  API Routes (Next.js Route Handlers)
  - Validate request bodies with Zod using `schema.safeParse(body)` instead of manual type checks.
  - Define schemas in `lib/validators.ts` (or a domain-specific module) and reuse across routes.
  - On parse failure, return 400 with the first validation error message: `parsed.error.errors[0]?.message`.
  - Use `.transform()` and `.refine()` for normalization (e.g. URL prepending `https://`, trimming strings) before business logic.
  - Avoid casting `body as { ... }`; always validate with Zod for type safety and consistent error handling.

  Logging
  - Always use `@offerpulse/lib` logger (`import { logger } from "@offerpulse/lib"`) instead of `console.log`, `console.warn`, or `console.error`.
  - Use `logger.debug` for development/troubleshooting, `logger.info` for notable events, `logger.warn` for recoverable issues, `logger.error` for failures.
  - In backend API routes, webhooks, and server functions, include sufficient debug logs for observability:
    - Log at entry: e.g. `logger.debug("Handler received", { key: value })` with request/event identifiers.
    - Log key decisions and branches: e.g. when skipping or short-circuiting, log the reason and relevant IDs.
    - Log before/after important operations: DB writes, external API calls, state changes.
    - Log success outcomes: e.g. `logger.info("Operation completed", { id, status })`.
    - Log errors with context: `logger.error("Operation failed", error)` including the error object.
  - Prefer structured context objects (e.g. `{ type, id, userId }`) over string concatenation for easier filtering and debugging.

  Environment Variables (apps/marketing/env.ts)
  - Use @t3-oss/env-core for type-safe, validated env vars. Define schema in apps/marketing/env.ts.
  - Split into server (API keys, secrets) and client (NEXT_PUBLIC_*). Never expose server vars to client.
  - Use createEnv with runtimeEnv: process.env and emptyStringAsUndefined: true.
  - Validate server vars with Zod (e.g. STRIPE_SECRET_KEY starts with sk_, RESEND_API_KEY with re_).
  - Import env from @/env (or apps/marketing/env.ts) only in server code (API routes, server components, server actions).
  - Keep .env.local.example in sync with the env schema; never commit .env or .env.local.
  - All env vars used by apps/marketing must be declared in turbo.json globalEnv so Turborepo passes them through and Vercel builds succeed.

  Vercel + Turborepo Deployment
  - Declare all server-side env vars from env.ts in turbo.json globalEnv (STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET, STRIPE_PRICE_LOOKUP_KEY, RESEND_API_KEY, AIRTABLE_API_KEY, AIRTABLE_BASE_ID, etc.).
  - This ensures Turborepo includes them in the build environment and cache key, preventing missing-env build failures on Vercel.
  - Configure the same vars in Vercel project settings for each app (marketing, app).

  Linear Issue Tracking
  - When a new feature or plan is requested, always check if there is an existing Linear issue in team ID `PULSE` for project ID `44127ff5-1ae5-4af4-80de-25e6144322cd` (OfferPulse MVP). If none exists, create one.
  - Always assign the issues to `chipz`
  - Create labels or assign relevant labels to the issue
  - For plans that involve multiple todos: create a parent issue first, then create sub-issues for each todo and assign them as children of the parent (e.g. via `parentId` when creating sub-issues).

  Development Server
  - DO NOT run `pnpm dev`, `pnpm dev:app`, or `pnpm dev:marketing` to test or confirm changes.
  - Use type checking (`pnpm type-check`) and linting (`pnpm lint`) to verify code correctness.
  - Assume the user will test changes in their own development environment.
  - Only start dev servers if the user explicitly requests it.
  